   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Parallel lines"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What are parallel lines?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Parallel lines** are** straight lines **with the **same gradient**\n\n  - Two parallel lines will **never meet**\n  \n    - They just stay side-by-side forever\n- The **equation** of the line **parallel** to *y*  = *mx*  + *c*  is ***y*****  = *****mx*****  + *****d***\n\n  - $y=2x+1$ and $y=2x+5$ are parallel\n  - $y=2x+1$ and $y=3x+1$ are **not** parallel"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find the equation of a parallel line?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- For example, to find the** equation** of the line **parallel** to *y * = 2*x * + 1 which **passes through** the **point **(3, 14)\n\n  - write the parallel line as *y*  = 2*x*  + *d*\n  \n    - using the same gradient of 2\n  - **substitute** *x*  = 3 and *y*  = 14 into this** **equation\n  \n    - 14 = 2 × 3 + *d*\n    - 14 = 6 + *d*\n  - **solve** to **find *****d***\n  \n    - *d * = 8\n  - The equation is *y * = 2*x*  + 8"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point (2, 1), which is parallel to the straight line $y=3x+7$.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n> *Parallel means the gradient will be the same*\n*Use **y**  = **mx ** + **d  **where **m ** = 3*\n\n$y=3x+d$\n\n> *Substitute in **x**  = 2 and **y**  = 1*\n\n$1=3\\times2+d$\n\n> *Simplify the equation*\n\n$1=6+d$\n\n> *Solve the equation to find **d ** (by subtracting 6 from both sides)*\n\n$-5=d$\n\n> *Write out the final answer in the form **y**  = **mx ** + **d *\n\n$y=3x-5$"
  }
 ]
}